-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Boundary Cells:** Failure to replace neighbor velocities with obstacle velocities leads to incorrect divergence near solid objects, violating the no-penetration condition [S2, unofficial].
-- **Precision:** Using floating-point arithmetic for divergence accumulation may introduce small errors; standard float32 is typically sufficient for real-time simulation [G].
-- **Grid Boundaries:** Voxels on the edge of the simulation domain may lack neighbors. The sources imply handling via obstacle velocity or zero-padding, but explicit edge handling logic is not detailed in the provided notes [S1, S2, unofficial].
+**Parameters**:
+- `0.5`: The scalar multiplier representing $\frac{1}{2\Delta x}$ in index space [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases**:
+- **Boundary Cells**: The method explicitly checks if neighbors are boundary cells. If so, it substitutes the obstacle velocity [S2, unofficial].
+- **Inactive Voxels**: The iteration is over active voxels only. Neighbors that are inactive (not in the grid) are treated as boundaries [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Precision**: Standard floating-point arithmetic is used. No specific precision requirements are noted beyond standard grid computations [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of active voxels. Each voxel requires 6 neighbor lookups and constant arithmetic operations [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the resulting divergence grid [G].
-- **Implementation:** The method is implemented in HLSL pixel shaders for real-time simulation [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple, efficient, and directly applicable to fluid simulation pressure projection [G].
-- **Limitations:** First-order accuracy in space (central difference is second-order accurate for the derivative, but the stencil is minimal). Sensitive to grid alignment and boundary condition implementation [G].
+- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of active voxels, as each active voxel requires 6 neighbor lookups and constant-time arithmetic [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the output grid [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance**: Not given in the sources.
+- **Strengths**: Handles complex fluid-solid interactions via obstacle velocity boundary conditions [S2, unofficial]. Simple and efficient stencil-based computation [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: Only computes divergence; does not solve the projection step itself (which is a separate kernel [S2, unofficial]). Accuracy is limited to first-order central differences [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from real-time fluid simulation techniques, specifically those described in "Real-Time Simulation and Rendering of 3D Fluids" [S2, unofficial]. It is a standard component in grid-based fluid solvers to compute the divergence field required for the Helmholtz-Hodge decomposition [G].
+The method is part of the GPU Gems 3 chapter on Real-Time Simulation and Rendering of 3D Fluids, which details GPU-based fluid simulation techniques [S2, unofficial]. It is also implemented in the Lyr.jl Julia library for volume rendering and simulation [S1, unofficial]. The approach builds on the shared principles of finite difference stencils used in [Second Derivative Computation](6353-second-derivative-computation.md) [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
